# Mutation plan for user creation and the two e-mail-token sends.
#
#   scripts/mutate-batch.sh scripts/mutations/user-creates.plan
#
# The decisions worth mutating here are the ones a reader could plausibly get backwards and whose
# wrong answer is a *successful* response:
#
#   * `IsEmpty`'s negation, which is the difference between a new signup being a plain member and
#     being a system administrator;
#   * the open-server gate's `&&`, whose `||` closes signup on a server that allows it;
#   * the locale reset in both directions — never firing keeps `zz`, always firing discards `fr`
#     — and the *setting* it resets to;
#   * the password guard's `AuthService == ""`, whose inversion admits a one-character password;
#   * `CheckEmailDomain`'s empty-list case, which is "allow" and reads like "deny";
#   * which query parameter forwards, and whether it forwards at all: a create that served the
#     `?t=` branch would allocate an account Go also allocates;
#   * `SanitizeInput`'s admin argument in both directions — one lets an anonymous signup verify
#     its own address, the other discards a system admin's;
#   * the hardened-mode rewrite, which turns three 400s into a 200 and is off on this stack;
#   * `SanitizeProfile(user, true)` on the verify-member reply.
#
# One line was replaced after the first pass. `app-locale-default-reads-the-wrong-setting`
# substituted `restrict_creation_to_domains`, which is `""` on this stack — and `User::PreSave`
# repairs an empty locale to `DefaultLocale`, which is also `"en"`. Right answer and wrong answer
# coincided and the mutation SURVIVED a suite that was in fact watching. `"de"` is a different
# *valid* locale and is caught.
#
# What no mutation here can show: that the reset reads `DefaultClientLocale` rather than a literal
# `"en"`. The setting is `"en"` on this stack, so Go and this port would agree either way. The
# config read is pinned by `Config`'s own fixture test instead.
#
# NOT mutated, and why: `is_first_user_account`'s `include_deleted: true`. Nothing reachable
# through the REST API produces the input that separates it from `false` — it would need a
# database whose every user row is soft-deleted — so the line would be a guaranteed survivor and
# a survivor that says nothing. It is pinned by its doc comment instead.
#
# The last two lines are the no-op controls and must SURVIVE.
store-is-empty-answers-backwards	crates/mm-store/src/user_store.rs	let empty = !has_rows.unwrap_or(false);	let empty = has_rows.unwrap_or(false);	api	user_creates
